meta {
  name: confirm-payment
  type: http
  seq: 9
}

post {
  url: {{base_url}}/api/admin/v1/orders/{{order_uid}}/payment/confirm
  body: json
  auth: bearer
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{access_token}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "reference": "UTR123456789",
    "note": "cash at counter"
  }
}

tests {
  test("responds 200", function () {
    expect(res.status).to.equal(200)
  })

  test("the payment is now paid", function () {
    expect(res.body.data.status).to.equal("paid")
  })

  test("and a served order closed itself", function () {
    // Settling an order that is already `served` advances it to `completed` through the state
    // machine, so the audit log is identical to a staff member tapping it. Verify with get-order.
    expect(res.body.data.paid_at).to.be.a("string")
  })
}

docs {
  # Confirm payment (staff)

The settlement path for cash and for static UPI, which cannot confirm itself. **This records that a
human saw the money** -- the same trust model as cash -- and the acting staff member is recorded on
the payment (docs/DECISIONS.md D2).

Two behaviours to note:

- **Send it twice.** The second call answers `TX_PAY_002` (409). Double settlement is an explicit
  refusal, never a silent second write.
- **A served order closes itself.** Settling an order that is already `served` advances it to
  `completed` through the state machine, so the audit log looks the same as if staff had tapped it.
}
